# Original frontend observations, isolated profile v7

The v13 discrepancy comes from using graph-projected edges as if they were
original frontend observations. `scanner._reusable_path_edges` selects a previous
public edge; `symbol_graph._candidate_ids` recognizes its concrete target CID as
one current candidate; `resolve_edge_targets` writes `resolution=definite` over
the earlier `finite_may` value. A new inventory also requires recomputing the
candidate set from its original lexical or module spelling. Reconstructing that
spelling alone is insufficient: graph projection can degrade original exact
confidence, and pytest unification can change original symbol identity/facets.

The immediate observed consumer is `_edge_limitations` in `semantic_index/explain.py`:
the v13 mismatch omits the specific `finite_may_target:<edge_id>` explanation,
while retaining `confidence:conservative`. Edge and derived-edge CIDs change,
which changes actual Merkle blocks and roots. These observations do not establish
an execution-authorization or execution-soundness failure.

The v6 guard rescans a file when an eligible prior edge was projected from a
lexical/module/fixture spelling to a different concrete target. Its full v14
comparison and native regression remain separate, including the intentionally
failed expectation that an unchanged projected path would skip analysis.

Profile v7 adds `frontend_observations.py` and changes only its isolated scanner.
Before pytest unification and graph projection, a producer records the original
Python symbol records and edges actually consumed by the scanner, or its fatal
diagnostics. It stores all parsed source paths in a canonical JSON sidecar.
Unconsumed frontend convenience fields are not claimed to be archived. The
sidecar is excluded from the public semantic state and bundle identity.

An incremental caller must provide exact sidecar bytes, their expected native
content CID from the accepted producer receipt, an accepted parent state and an
explicit frozen runtime-profile SHA-256. The closed envelope also binds parent
state CID, repository, namespace, scanner/schema identity and per-path source
CID. Typed native symbol/edge restoration rechecks native identities and source
ownership. The caller must independently enforce the declared runtime manifest;
the full qualification hashes every enrolled runtime source before and after
each worker. A CID is an integrity binding to this honest producer artifact,
not a proof that arbitrary supplied observations follow from source bytes.

The scanner verifies every current supplied source against the current snapshot
before consulting cached observations. Changed files are reanalyzed. Missing
per-path observations trigger reanalysis. Present but incorrectly bound or
malformed sidecars fail closed. Pytest analysis/unification and whole-inventory
graph resolution always rerun. Original confidence, lexical targets and metadata
are retained instead of being guessed from graph output. Enabling sidecar
production without an original sidecar performs fresh Python analysis, including
previously reusable paths; this bootstrap cost is charged. Callers that do not
enable the sidecar continue to use the v6 guard.

`frontend_observations_validation_v7/summary.json` contains ten sequential
inventory/source transitions, fresh native state restoration, full native bundle
verification and byte equality for every actual block and sidecar. It also
contains sixteen custody/source negatives or explicit safe fallbacks. In every
inventory transition the unchanged ambiguous caller is actually reused. The
import control loses its target and becomes conservative, then regains an exact
target and exact confidence from the retained original observation. Pytest
fixture/configuration and opaque-source changes are included. These constructed
controls qualify the mechanism; they do not measure full-repository economy.

The source profile, materializer, driver, protocol and runtime manifest are
versioned independently of v5/v6, retained v13/v14, and live Requests profiles.
No provider calls or protected task payloads are used.
